{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "98",
    "case_summary": "A 60-year-old woman presented with swelling in the left periorbital region and a frontal headache on the same side. Her medical history included sinus surgery performed 9 months prior. General ENT examination showed no signs of recurrent nasal polyposis. Contrast-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) indicated involvement of the left ethmoid and frontal sinuses. Computed tomography (CT) scans corroborated these findings. The patient underwent transfacial sinus surgery according to the RITTER-JANSEN method, which included a median drainage with a placeholder for 3 weeks. The bony wall of the frontal sinus and the dura remained unaffected.\n\nHistopathological examination showed lobules and fascicles of uniform partly spindle-shaped tumor cells with oval nuclei and intranuclear clear inclusions. Infrequently, whorl formation and psammoma bodies were present. Cellular atypia, necrosis, or increased mitotic activity was not detected. Immunohistochemistry revealed weak expression of epithelial membrane antigen (EMA), vimentin and S-100.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
